drm: h713-afbd: the ring's source rectangle is what the plane reads

The window of 0133b turned out to be a window in the RING, not on the panel.
Measured on the HY310 with the debug kernel (dev20, 22.09.2026, 1080p60
source): at `h713-tv ctl zoom 80` the DE picture scaler's window 0x05180034
went 0x07800438 -> 0x06000360 and the Proc node 0x05140124/0x05140128 to
1536/864, while INCAP's active geometry 0x06940874 stayed 1920x1080 and its
rowbyte 0x06940924 stayed 0x00780078, 1920 bytes a line. So the firmware took
words 31..34, re-ran its window chain and scaled its picture down to 1536x864
- and wrote it into a ring it did not re-lay-out. The fresh picture lies in the
top left corner of a 1920-byte-pitch ring and the rest of that ring is the last
frame that filled it. The plane went on reading all of it, so the wall showed a
shrunken picture with a band of stale pixels: "distorted", correctly.

The ring's layout stands still because the capture node is committed on its
own. The firmware's window manager walks five nodes (Cap, NR, DETN, Proc,
Panel) and commits each only if its own bypass byte is clear; T1 measured the
other direction of the same thing, a Cap node written and a Proc node skipped
(AP3c section 5, the T1 4b case). Here Proc and the scaler moved and Cap did
not, which is exactly the state the registers show.

What follows is one line of KMS: the part of the ring that carries a picture is
the plane's source rectangle. In passthrough it may now be smaller than the
framebuffer - starting at the ring's first byte, whole pixels, an even number
of lines - and the geometry, block and crop registers are programmed from it
while the stride stays the ring's. That is a crop, the one the AFBD's crop
registers are for, and it costs no register this driver did not already write.

Two things deliberately stay as they are. The record still carries the ring's
geometry and not the crop: the firmware rebuilds its capture window chain from
that record and derives what it scales to from the frame size and the window
words together, so a cropped frame size would carry the window ratio twice and
shrink the fresh area a second time. And the capture driver's ring geometry
(0136f) is untouched: the rowbyte and INCAP active did not move, so V4L2 goes
on reporting 1920x1080 with a 1920-byte pitch, which is what the ring really
is. The window is not a property of the signal - it is what a client asked
for, and the client that asked knows it.

What the HY310 sees: nothing, until somebody zooms. A plane whose source
rectangle is its whole framebuffer - which is what the check demanded until now
and what h713-tv sends at zoom 100 - programs the same four registers with the
same values and publishes the same record, word for word. Without a ring the
source rectangle must still be the whole framebuffer; that rule only moved from
the common block into the branch that owns it.

Not measured: whether the firmware writes the scaled picture to the ring's base
or at the window's own offset inside the ring. The registers that moved carry
sizes only, and the capture writes from the buffer base every other time we
have watched it, so the base is what this patch assumes. If the bench run shows
the picture shifted instead of complete, the answer is SRC_X/SRC_Y in h713-tv
- one line, no kernel change.

Follow-up to 0133b (same file, the other half of the same window); needs
nothing else.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff -ruN a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
@@ -924,9 +924,10 @@
  * programs it for the signal it detected, in the same UpdateWce that lays the
  * ring out (doku/91 Punkt 2, step 6 of the stock chain). So in passthrough a
  * source smaller than the panel is accepted and, as far as KMS is concerned,
- * shown in the plane's destination rectangle -- src is the whole framebuffer,
- * dst the whole CRTC unless somebody asks for a window -- and
- * the plane programs source 0 for the framebuffer's size and nothing else.
+ * shown in the plane's destination rectangle -- dst the whole CRTC unless
+ * somebody asks for a window, src the whole ring unless the firmware scales
+ * into it -- and the plane programs source 0 for that source rectangle and
+ * nothing else.
  * That the composition really follows is inferred from the stock chain and
  * from INCAP following, not yet measured on the wall; the acceptance in
  * doku/91 Punkt 2 is where that gets measured. A source larger than the panel
@@ -992,8 +993,8 @@
 	    fb->width < H713_VIDEO_BLOCK ||
 	    fb->height < H713_VIDEO_BLOCK ||
 	    new_state->src_x || new_state->src_y ||
-	    new_state->src_w != fb->width << 16 ||
-	    new_state->src_h != fb->height << 16 ||
+	    new_state->src_w > fb->width << 16 ||
+	    new_state->src_h > fb->height << 16 ||
 	    new_state->crtc_x < 0 || new_state->crtc_y < 0 ||
 	    new_state->crtc_w < H713_WINDOW_MIN ||
 	    new_state->crtc_h < H713_WINDOW_MIN ||
@@ -1020,11 +1021,27 @@
 			return -EOPNOTSUPP;
 		if (fb->pitches[0] != ALIGN(fb->width, H713_VIDEO_BLOCK))
 			return -EINVAL;
+		/*
+		 * The source rectangle is the part of the ring that carries a
+		 * picture, and it starts at the ring's first byte: the capture
+		 * writes from the buffer base with the pitch it was laid out
+		 * with. Whole pixels and an even number of lines, because the
+		 * chroma crop is half the height; a width that is not a whole
+		 * block truncates its last columns as a 1366-wide ring does
+		 * today (0131), which is the client's business.
+		 */
+		if ((new_state->src_w | new_state->src_h) & 0xffff ||
+		    (new_state->src_h >> 16) % 2 ||
+		    new_state->src_w < H713_VIDEO_BLOCK << 16 ||
+		    new_state->src_h < H713_VIDEO_BLOCK << 16)
+			return -EINVAL;
 		return 0;
 	}
 
 	if (fb->width != mode->hdisplay || fb->height != mode->vdisplay ||
-	    fb->pitches[0] != fb->width)
+	    fb->pitches[0] != fb->width ||
+	    new_state->src_w != fb->width << 16 ||
+	    new_state->src_h != fb->height << 16)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	y = drm_fb_dma_get_gem_addr(fb, new_state, 0);
@@ -1051,8 +1068,19 @@
 	new_state = drm_atomic_get_new_plane_state(state, plane);
 	s = to_h713_plane_state(new_state);
 	fb = new_state->fb;
-	w = fb->width;
-	hgt = fb->height;
+	/*
+	 * What this plane reads is the source rectangle, not the whole
+	 * framebuffer: the firmware scales its picture INTO the ring and
+	 * leaves the ring's layout alone (dev20, 22.09.2026), so a window
+	 * leaves the fresh picture in the ring's top left corner and the rest
+	 * of the ring standing at the last frame that filled it. The crop is
+	 * the geometry, block and crop registers below, with the stride left
+	 * at the ring's - which is what makes it a crop. Without a ring the
+	 * source rectangle is the whole framebuffer and these are fb->width
+	 * and fb->height as before.
+	 */
+	w = drm_rect_width(&new_state->src) >> 16;
+	hgt = drm_rect_height(&new_state->src) >> 16;
 	nv16 = fb->format->format == DRM_FORMAT_NV16;
 	ring = s->hdmi_ring;
 
@@ -1071,7 +1099,16 @@
 		 */
 		if (nv16)
 			writel(0, h->regs + AFBD_VIDEO_C_STRIDE);
-		published = h713_afbd_publish_video_info(h, w, hgt,
+		/*
+		 * The record keeps the RING's geometry, not the crop: the
+		 * firmware rebuilds its capture chain from it
+		 * (ConvertFrameInfo2SignalInfo) and derives what it scales to
+		 * from the frame size and the window words together, so a
+		 * cropped frame size would carry the window ratio twice and
+		 * shrink the fresh area again.
+		 */
+		published = h713_afbd_publish_video_info(h, fb->width,
+							 fb->height,
 							 fb->pitches[0], ring,
 							 &new_state->dst,
 							 s->aspect);
